% --- b) Abstract ---
\begin{abstract}
This report details the implementation and comparative evaluation of a monolingual information retrieval (IR) system on the standard TREC Associated Press (AP) 88-90 collection. We studied the influence of text preprocessing (Porter stemming vs. baseline), query length (short vs. long), and the weighting model (QLD vs. BM25). The system was developed in Python with Pyserini under Java 21. The best-identified base configuration (Baseline, Long Queries, BM25) achieved a MAP of 0.2205. Stemming proved to be less effective than the baseline (max MAP of 0.1778). An enhancement technique using pseudo-relevance feedback, RM3, applied to the best baseline configuration, achieved a final MAP of 0.2948, demonstrating a significant relative improvement (+34%). The analysis confirms the superiority of BM25 and long queries, and the effectiveness of RM3 on a strong baseline.
\end{abstract}

% --- c) Keywords ---
% Mettez 3 à 5 mots clés ici. Suggestions :
Keywords: Information Retrieval, TREC, Pyserini, BM25, QLD, Stemming, Query Expansion, RM3.

% --- d) Introduction ---
\section{Introduction}
Information Retrieval (IR) aims to retrieve relevant documents that satisfy an information need, expressed as a query, from within a large collection. This field is crucial given the explosion in the volume of available textual data. This project falls within this framework, focusing on monolingual (English) search on the Associated Press (AP) 88-90 collection, a reference collection from the TREC (Text REtrieval Conference) evaluation campaigns organized by NIST \citep{Harman1995TREC}. % \citep{trecWebsite}

The specific objectives of this work are threefold:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item To implement a complete IR pipeline, including indexing and search, using modern open-source tools (Pyserini/Lucene).
    \item To quantitatively evaluate the impact on performance (measured by MAP and P@10) of key methodological choices: the use of linguistic preprocessing (Porter stemming vs. baseline), the variation of query length (titles vs. titles+descriptions), and the comparison of two weighting models (QLD vs. BM25).
    \item To implement and evaluate a standard enhancement technique, the pseudo-relevance feedback model RM3, to quantify its potential contribution on the best-identified base configuration.
\end{enumerate}
This report details our experimental methodology, the results obtained during the different phases, a discussion of these results, and a qualitative error analysis on selected queries. It is structured as follows: Section \ref{sec:etat_art} presents a brief overview of related work. Section \ref{sec:methodo} describes our methodological approach. Section \ref{sec:donnees} details the data collection. Section \ref{sec:eval} presents and discusses the results of our evaluations. Finally, Section \ref{sec:conclusion} concludes this work and suggests future directions.
\clearpage
% --- e) Related Work ---
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:etat_art}
Modern information retrieval is built upon foundations established over several decades. The vector space model, where documents and queries are represented as vectors in a term space, was popularized by \citet{Salton1988TermWeighting}. This model allows for ranking documents based on their similarity (e.g., cosine) to the query. An essential component of this model is term weighting, where the TF-IDF (Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency) scheme has become a de facto standard \citep{Salton1988TermWeighting}.

The probabilistic model offers an alternative, seeking to estimate the probability that a document is relevant to a query. The Okapi BM25 model \citep{Robertson1995Okapi} is one of the most effective and robust ranking models for ad-hoc search. It sophisticatedly integrates term frequency (TF), inverse document frequency (IDF), and document length. Another important probabilistic model is the language modeling approach to IR (QLD), where one estimates P(Query | Document), using smoothing to avoid zero probabilities \citep{Manning2008IRBook}. % \citep{nieIRBook}

Text preprocessing (tokenization, lowercasing, stopword removal, stemming/lemmatization) aims to normalize the text. While lemmatization is linguistically more precise, stemming (e.g., Porter) is often used \citep{Manning2008IRBook}. The choice impacts the precision/recall trade-off. % \citep{dataCampStemLemma}, \citep{jurafskyMartinSLP3}

Query expansion aims to improve initial queries. Pseudo-relevance feedback (PRF), such as RM3 \citep{AbdulJaleel2004PRF}, uses the top-ranked documents to enrich the query. Other approaches include lexical resources (WordNet \citep{Miller1995WordNet}), word embeddings (Word Embeddings \citep{Mikolov2013WordEmbeddings}), or LLMs for re-ranking \citep{Nogueira2019BERTReRank} or query generation \citep{Ma2023QueryGenerationLLM}. \citep{Devlin2019BERT}, \citep{stanfordCS224n}

Reproducibility is facilitated by tools like Pyserini \citep{Lin2021Pyserini} and standard collections like TREC AP \citep{Harman1995TREC}. Conferences such as SIGIR \citep{sigirWebsite} and archives like the ACL Anthology \citep{aclAnthologyWebsite} are key references.

% --- f) Methodology ---
\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:methodo}
Our IR system is implemented in Python 3.11 on Google Colaboratory, using Pyserini (version ) and Lucene under OpenJDK 21.

\subsection{General IR Steps}
The process followed includes the classic steps (Figure \ref{fig:pipeline}):
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Preprocessing and Indexing:} Preparing documents and building the index. Comparison of two strategies: baseline (standard Lucene) and stemming (Porter + NLTK stopwords).
    \item \textbf{Search:} Querying the index with short/long queries and weighting models (BM25/QLD).
    \item \textbf{Evaluation:} Quantitative measurement (MAP, P@10) with Qrels.
    \item \textbf{Enhancement:} Application of RM3 on the best base configuration.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{figure}[htbp] % Placement 'here', 'top', 'bottom', 'page'
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=1.3\columnwidth]{pipeline.png}
  \caption{Diagram of the Information Retrieval pipeline. Note: I used NLTK and not SpaCy. Source: https://gitlab.com/Darwin99/indexation-with-pylucene}
  \label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure}
\clearpage
\subsection{Detailed Implementation}
\paragraph{Indexing:} The AP88-90 collection (`AP.tar`) was extracted (including `.gz` decompression) and formatted into JSONL. Two Lucene indexes were built:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Baseline Index:} Direct indexing. Size: 825M.
    \item \textbf{Preprocessed (Stemming) Index:} Indexing after tokenization, lowercasing, stop word removal, and Porter stemming (NLTK), with the `--pretokenized` option. Size: 1.6G.
\end{itemize}
Lucene options enabled: `-storePositions`, `-storeDocvectors`, `-storeRaw`.

\paragraph{Base Search:} Short (`<title>`) and long (`<title>` + `<desc>`) queries. Queries were stemmed for the corresponding index. Search was performed with `LuceneSearcher` (k=1000), using BM25 \texttt{(setbm25) and QLD (setqld) models. 8 runs were generated sequentially.}

\paragraph{Enhancement with RM3:} The RM3 method (\texttt{set\_rm3}) was applied on \texttt{baseline\_long\_bm25}.\\
Standard parameters:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{fb\_docs=10}
  \item \texttt{fb\_terms=10}
  \item \texttt{original\_query\_weight=0.5}
\end{itemize}



\begin{figure}[htbp] % Placement 'here', 'top', 'bottom', 'page'
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=1.0\columnwidth]{indexation.png}
  \caption{Indexing. Source: https://gitlab.com/Darwin99/indexation-with-pylucene}
  \label{fig:indexation}
\end{figure}


\paragraph{RM3 Enhancement:}
RM3 was applied to the best base configuration, \texttt{baseline\_long\_bm25}.\\
Table \ref{tab:rm3_comparison_final} presents the performance comparison.


% --- TABLEAU COMPARATIF RM3 (Basé sur Baseline - Formaté avec booktabs) ---
\begin{table}[htbp] % Placement 'here', 'top', 'bottom', 'page'
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcc} % 3 colonnes alignées à gauche, au centre, au centre
\toprule
\textbf{Configuration} & \textbf{MAP} & \textbf{P@10} \\
\midrule
Baseline + Long + BM25 (Base) & 0.2205 & 0.4765 \\
Baseline + Long + BM25 + RM3 & \textbf{0.2948} & \textbf{0.5078} \\
\midrule
Relative Improvement & +33.7\% & +6.6\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Performance comparison before and after RM3 enhancement on the Baseline+Long+BM25 base.}
\label{tab:rm3_comparison_final}
\end{table}
\section{Data and Resources}
\label{sec:donnees}
\subsection{TREC AP 88-90 Collection}
Associated Press collection (1988-1990). 242,918 documents. SGML-like format. Source: TREC/NIST \citep{trecWebsite}.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  % Assurez-vous que 'TREC88-90.png' est dans le même dossier
  \includegraphics[width=0.6\columnwidth]{TREC88-90.png}
  \caption{Key statistics of the collection. Source: https://gitlab.com/Darwin99/indexation-with-pylucene}
  \label{fig:trecstats}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Topics (Queries)}
150 TREC topics (1-150) with `<num>`, `<title>`, and `<desc>` fields.

\subsection{Relevance Judgments (Qrels)}
`qrels.*.AP8890.txt` files. Evaluation is based on the 51 judged queries.

\subsection{Software Tools}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Python 3.11 (Google Colaboratory)
    \item Pyserini ([**Installed Version**]) / Lucene
    \item Java OpenJDK 21
    \item NLTK (PorterStemmer, stopwords, tokenizers)
    \item Pytrec\_eval (evaluation)
    \item Pandas (results manipulation)
\end{itemize}

% --- h) Evaluations and Results ---
\section{Evaluations and Results}
\label{sec:eval}
\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}
Evaluation was conducted with `pytrec_eval` on 51 judged queries. Metrics: MAP@1000 and P@10.

\subsection{Results of the Base System (Stemming vs. Baseline)}
The performance of the 8 base configurations is presented in Tables \ref{tab:map_results_final} and \ref{tab:p10_results_final}, and visualized (potentially with different/earlier data) in Figures \ref{fig:map_comparison_chart} and \ref{fig:p10_comparison_chart}.

