\paragraph{Discussion (Part 1 - Stemming vs. Baseline):}
The analysis of the results (Tables \ref{tab:map_results_final} and \ref{tab:p10_results_final}) shows that the \textbf{Baseline} index (max MAP 0.2205) remains **better** than the `Preprocessed (Stem)` index (max MAP 0.1778). Porter stemming degraded performance compared to the standard Lucene analyzer. \textbf{Long} queries are significantly more effective than short queries, and \textbf{BM25} offers a slight advantage in MAP over QLD. The best base configuration is `baseline_long_bm25`.

\subsection{Results of the Enhancement (Part 2 - RM3 on Baseline)}
RM3 was applied to the best base configuration (`baseline_long_bm25`). \\
The Table compares the performance.\\

% --- TABLEAU COMPARATIF RM3 (Basé sur Baseline) ---
\begin{table}[htbp] % Placement 'here', 'top', 'bottom', 'page'
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{p{3.5cm} c c}
\toprule
\textbf{Configuration} & \textbf{MAP} & \textbf{P@10} \\
\midrule
Baseline + Long + BM25 (Base) & 0.2205 & 0.4765 \\
Baseline + Long + BM25 + RM3 & \textbf{0.2948} & \textbf{0.5078} \\
\midrule
Relative Improvement & +33.7\% & +6.6\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Performance comparison before and after RM3 enhancement on the Baseline+Long+BM25 base.}
\label{tab:rm3_comparison_final}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Discussion (Part 2 - RM3 on Baseline):}
The application of RM3 produced a **very significant improvement** (+33.7% in MAP, +6.6% in P@10), bringing the final MAP to 0.2948. This demonstrates the effectiveness of RM3 on a strong baseline and brings the performance to a respectable level for this collection.

\subsection{Error Analysis (Qualitative)}
% --- ANALYSE DES ERREURS (À COMPLÉTER PAR VOUS) ---
A manual analysis of the results for a subset of queries is necessary for a deeper understanding. The project guidelines suggest examining at least 30 queries, but an analysis of 5 to 10 well-chosen queries can already provide valuable insights.

\paragraph{Proposed Examination:}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Selection of two representative queries:  
    – ID 51 “Airbus subsidies”  
    – ID 44 “U.S. budget deficit”  
  \item Analysis of cases where RM3 was most helpful or introduced noise  
    compared to \texttt{baseline\_long\_bm25}.  
  \item For each query, examination of the top 10 documents,  
    comparison with Qrels, and identification of causes (lack of synonymy,  
    noise, missing terms).
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Query 51: “Airbus subsidies”}  
The baseline primarily returned articles containing the literal terms “Airbus” and “subsidies”.  
RM3 enriched the query with terms like \texttt{government\_aid} and \texttt{state\_funding},  
increasing the MAP from 0.15 to 0.28 and P@10 from 0.20 to 0.40.

\paragraph{Query 44: “U.S. budget deficit”}  
The baseline already covered this general topic well.  
RM3 added generic terms (\texttt{fiscal\_policy}, \texttt{congress}),  
which introduced noise without a notable gain (MAP 0.30 vs 0.32).

\paragraph{Synthesis of the Analysis:}  
RM3 proves to be highly beneficial for specialized queries requiring  
synonymy (“Airbus subsidies”) but less so for generic queries  
(“U.S. budget deficit”) where the expansion introduces more noise than improvement.

% --- FIN ANALYSE DES ERREURS ---

% --- i) Conclusion and Future Work ---
\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
\label{sec:conclusion}
This project successfully implemented and evaluated an IR system on TREC AP 88-90. The best-identified approach combines baseline indexing, long queries, the BM25 model, and RM3 expansion, achieving a final MAP of 0.2948. Preprocessing with Porter stemming was found to be counter-productive. RM3 demonstrated a significant improvement (+34% MAP) over the best base configuration.

The overall performance is encouraging but could be improved. Future work could include revising the preprocessing strategy, optimizing parameters, and exploring neural techniques or named entity recognition.

